1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,300
Your agent just ran fourteen steps.

2
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:07,650
Then it deleted your tests and overwrote
your config.

3
00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:10,800
Can you get them back?

4
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:14,950
Three parts: the problem, how it works,
the fix.

5
00:00:14,950 --> 00:00:19,850
Why can't git help? The third test was
never committed.

6
00:00:19,850 --> 00:00:23,300
Claude Code's docs: shell-only changes
aren't tracked.

7
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:27,250
So what would help? Think of a flight
recorder.

8
00:00:27,250 --> 00:00:31,500
It keeps every move, so you can replay it.

9
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:35,050
So how does rewindagents record your
agent?

10
00:00:35,050 --> 00:00:39,150
Every tool call goes into one hash-chained
log.

11
00:00:39,150 --> 00:00:43,600
Before each edit or shell command, files
are saved.

12
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:47,750
So which step would you rewind to?

13
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:50,950
Twelve, because thirteen is the delete.

14
00:00:50,950 --> 00:00:55,200
One command, and four files come back.

15
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:58,500
Even the rewind can be undone.

16
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:03,000
Where does the analogy break? Recorders
can't undo a crash.

17
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,950
Unlike a recorder, this one puts your
files back.

18
00:01:06,950 --> 00:01:11,200
Preview with diff. Take back one file with
--only.

19
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:16,350
You can now see every step and the change
it made.

20
00:01:16,350 --> 00:01:21,150
Up next: rewindagents init finds the seven
agents it supports.

21
00:01:21,150 --> 00:01:24,950
Apache-2.0. Standard library only. Not on
PyPI yet.

22
00:01:24,950 --> 00:01:30,000
Your editor has had undo for decades. Now
your agent does.
